10:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L17",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 23, 1861–July 17, 1862","place":"Fort Monroe and Hampton Roads, Virginia; Washington, D.C.; Missouri and Kentucky; the Department of the South in South Carolina, Georgia, and Florida; and the loyal slave states of Delaware, Maryland, Kentucky, and Missouri","result":"Self-emancipating people and Union field commanders forced the United States government to decide how its armies would treat people escaping slavery. Benjamin Butler’s refusal to return three men used on Confederate fortifications led to the “contraband” policy, but neither that policy nor the First Confiscation Act freed every enslaved person who reached United States lines. Congress prohibited military personnel from returning freedom seekers in March 1862. Lincoln revoked broader emancipation orders issued by John C. Frémont and David Hunter while unsuccessfully urging the loyal slave states to adopt gradual, state-directed emancipation with federal compensation. By July 1862, field practice, legislation, and military necessity had moved federal policy substantially closer to presidential emancipation","facts":[{"label":"Episode scope","value":"The federal policy path from “contraband” to compensated emancipation","note":"The episode follows events from May 1861 through Lincoln’s final July 1862 appeal to border-state representatives. The Second Confiscation Act and Lincoln’s presidential proclamation are reserved for the next episode."},{"label":"Enslaved population in 1860","value":"3,953,760 people in the United States","note":"The episode rounds the total to almost four million. The 1860 census map counted 3,350,343 enslaved people in the fifteen slaveholding states shown on the map; the national total also included enslaved people elsewhere."},{"label":"Deep South total","value":"More than 2.3 million enslaved people in seven seceding states","note":"The state totals for South Carolina, Mississippi, Louisiana, Alabama, Florida, Georgia, and Texas sum to 2,312,046."},{"label":"Largest state proportions","value":"57.2% in South Carolina and 55.1% in Mississippi","note":"In 1860 enslaved people were also 47.0% of Louisiana’s population, 45.1% of Alabama’s, 43.9% of Florida’s, and 43.7% of Georgia’s."},{"label":"Fort Monroe’s status","value":"A United States-held fort inside seceded Virginia","note":"Its position at Hampton Roads made it both a military base and a reachable place of refuge for people escaping nearby enslavement."},{"label":"The first three at Fort Monroe","value":"Frank Baker, Shepard Mallory, and James Townsend","note":"These are the names by which the three men are known today; the National Park Service notes that definitive contemporary identification records have not survived. They escaped after being forced to work on Confederate gun positions."},{"label":"Arrival and decision","value":"May 23–24, 1861","note":"The men arrived on May 23. Butler interviewed them the next day and refused a demand that they be returned to enslaver and Confederate colonel Charles K. Mallory."},{"label":"Butler’s legal reasoning","value":"Enemy property used for war could be retained as “contraband”","note":"Butler accepted slavery’s property-law premise for the limited purpose of denying labor to the rebellion. He also argued that Virginia could not claim the benefits of the Fugitive Slave Act while claiming to have left the United States."},{"label":"What the contraband decision did not do","value":"It did not issue a formal declaration of freedom","note":"The three men passed from an enslaver’s control into United States military custody and were hired to work at the fort. Their initiative protected them in practice, but their legal status remained unsettled."},{"label":"Administration approval","value":"Secretary of War Simon Cameron approved Butler’s policy on May 30","note":"General-in-Chief Winfield Scott and the Lincoln administration supported the refusal to surrender people reaching United States lines and the use of their labor."},{"label":"Refugee movement","value":"More than 1,000 freedom seekers near Fort Monroe by August 1861","note":"The policy followed, rather than initiated, self-emancipation. Men, women, and children continued crossing into United States-held territory, expanding the issue beyond Butler’s immediate labor needs."},{"label":"House resolution","value":"July 9, 1861: soldiers had no duty to capture and return fugitives","note":"The House resolution, urged by Representative Owen Lovejoy, expressed policy but was not yet the binding military prohibition Congress enacted in March 1862."},{"label":"First Confiscation Act","value":"Signed August 6, 1861","note":"The law authorized forfeiture of an enslaver’s claim when the enslaved person was required or permitted to work in support of the rebellion. It did not abolish slavery generally or automatically cover every person who reached United States lines."},{"label":"Additional Article of War","value":"Signed March 13, 1862","note":"Congress prohibited officers and other military or naval personnel from using forces under their command to return people escaping claimed service or labor. A court-martial conviction required dismissal from service."},{"label":"Frémont’s proclamation","value":"August 30, 1861, in Missouri","note":"Maj. Gen. John C. Frémont imposed martial law and declared enslaved people belonging to active Confederate supporters free. He acted without prior approval from Washington."},{"label":"Lincoln’s response to Frémont","value":"Ordered the emancipation clause conformed to the First Confiscation Act","note":"Lincoln first asked Frémont to modify the order on September 2. After Frémont declined to do so on his own authority, Lincoln publicly directed the change on September 11."},{"label":"Hunter’s proclamation","value":"May 9, 1862, in South Carolina, Georgia, and Florida","note":"Maj. Gen. David Hunter declared enslaved people in the Department of the South free. United States control in the department extended over only limited coastal areas."},{"label":"Lincoln’s response to Hunter","value":"Declared the emancipation provision void on May 19","note":"Lincoln stated that no commander had authority from the government to issue such a declaration and reserved to himself the decision whether emancipation had become a military necessity."},{"label":"Border-state constraint","value":"Delaware, Maryland, Kentucky, and Missouri remained in the United States","note":"Lincoln feared that unauthorized general emancipation could damage Unionist support in loyal slave states. Missouri was also the setting of Frémont’s order, making the issue immediate rather than hypothetical."},{"label":"Contradictory field practice","value":"Some officers sheltered freedom seekers while others excluded or returned them","note":"Orders by Henry W. Halleck and John A. Dix restricting entry to military lines illustrate why federal practice remained inconsistent before Congress imposed the March 1862 prohibition."},{"label":"Compensated-emancipation proposal","value":"Lincoln asked Congress for federal aid on March 6, 1862","note":"His proposed resolution offered federal financial assistance to any state that voluntarily adopted gradual abolition. Congress approved the joint resolution in April, but no loyal slave state adopted the program."},{"label":"Colonization in the proposal","value":"Voluntary emigration was paired with gradual emancipation","note":"Lincoln presented colonization as a way to reduce white resistance. Most Black Americans opposed leaving the country, and the wartime colonization projects attempted by the administration failed."},{"label":"Delaware test case","value":"1,798 enslaved people in the 1860 census","note":"Lincoln hoped the smallest slaveholding state would lead, but opposition was strong enough that supporters did not bring a compensated-emancipation proposal to a vote in its legislature."},{"label":"Final border-state appeal","value":"July 12, 1862","note":"Lincoln warned representatives that slavery would be destroyed by the continuing war without compensation. A majority response dated July 14 disputed his reasoning and declined immediate action, although its signers said their states could consider the proposal."},{"label":"Policy transition","value":"Congress enacted the Second Confiscation Act on July 17, 1862","note":"The episode stops just before discussing the act. Its emancipation and Black-enlistment provisions supplied part of the immediate legal setting for Lincoln’s July 22 draft proclamation."},{"label":"Agency and policy","value":"Freedom seekers made federal inaction impossible","note":"Baker, Mallory, Townsend, and thousands of others acted before national emancipation policy existed. Their arrival inside United States lines forced commanders, Congress, and the president to respond."},{"label":"Recommended book","value":"“The Fiery Trial” by Eric Foner","note":"The full title is “The Fiery Trial: Abraham Lincoln and American Slavery.” The hosts had also recommended it in episode 50."},{"label":"Language in this guide","value":"“Contraband” appears only as a period legal and administrative term","note":"The term treated human beings as seized property. This guide otherwise uses “enslaved people,” “freedom seekers,” and “self-emancipating people” to describe the people whose actions drove the policy change."}],"maps":[{"title":"Where field policy changed, 1861–1862","caption":"The most important field decisions occurred in distinct military jurisdictions. Butler retained freedom seekers at Fort Monroe in Virginia; Frémont issued a broader order in Missouri; Hunter did the same in the Department of the South; and Lincoln’s political concern centered on the loyal slave states. Locations and department boundaries are generalized, and the diagram is not to scale.","source":"National Park Service, Library of Congress, Abraham Lincoln Papers, and episode transcript; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep202-policy-geography.svg","alt":"Schematic map locating Fort Monroe, Missouri, Washington, the loyal slave states, and the Department of the South in the evolution of United States emancipation policy."},{"title":"From refuge to federal policy","caption":"Self-emancipation created the immediate problem. Military decisions, congressional legislation, and presidential action then developed in parallel. The arrows show chronological movement, not a claim that any single event alone caused the next.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Senate Historical Office, Library of Congress, National Archives, and Abraham Lincoln’s messages; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep202-policy-timeline.svg","alt":"Three-lane timeline of self-emancipation, military decisions, legislation, and presidential policy between May 1861 and July 1862."},{"title":"Arrival at Fortress Monroe","caption":"Frank Leslie’s Illustrated Newspaper published this composite engraving on June 8, 1861. Its central scene depicts African American freedom seekers entering Fort Monroe, while surrounding scenes show movement, reception, work, and camp life. It is a contemporary illustrated-news representation, not a photograph of the May 23 arrival.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below.","image":"/images/ep202-fort-monroe-arrivals.jpg","alt":"An 1861 newspaper engraving showing African American freedom seekers arriving at Fort Monroe and related scenes around the fort."}],"moments":[{"time":"1:12","audioTime":72,"title":"The episode restates the military case against slavery","detail":"Enslaved labor sustained Confederate agriculture, logistics, and fortification work, making slavery both a war resource and the subject of a growing attack by the United States."},{"time":"1:42","audioTime":102,"title":"The 1860 enslaved population is quantified","detail":"The census counted nearly four million enslaved people nationwide, with more than 2.3 million in the seven states of the Deep South."},{"time":"2:38","audioTime":158,"title":"Military necessity and justice meet political resistance","detail":"The hosts distinguish the case for weakening the Confederacy and ending oppression from the racism and political divisions that constrained policy in the loyal states."},{"time":"3:31","audioTime":211,"title":"The war changes northern calculations","detail":"Mounting casualties, military reverses, and a longer conflict weakened arguments that the United States could defeat the rebellion while leaving slavery untouched."},{"time":"4:32","audioTime":272,"title":"Self-emancipation turns debate into an operational question","detail":"As armies entered Confederate territory, enslaved people left enslavers and reached United States lines, requiring commanders to decide whether to shelter, employ, exclude, or return them."},{"time":"4:57","audioTime":297,"title":"The narrative returns to Fort Monroe","detail":"The fort at the tip of the Virginia Peninsula remained in United States hands and became the setting of the first widely influential “contraband” decision."},{"time":"5:11","audioTime":311,"title":"Three men arrive after forced fortification work","detail":"Frank Baker, Shepard Mallory, and James Townsend escaped to Fort Monroe on May 23, 1861, after being forced to labor on Confederate gun positions."},{"time":"5:28","audioTime":328,"title":"Butler’s prewar politics are established","detail":"Benjamin Butler had been a pro-Southern Massachusetts Democrat and had supported Jefferson Davis for the Democratic presidential nomination in 1860."},{"time":"6:20","audioTime":380,"title":"Butler refuses the return demand","detail":"A Confederate representative demanded the three men under the Fugitive Slave Act. Butler refused to restore laborers who had been used to build enemy fortifications."},{"time":"7:06","audioTime":426,"title":"The contraband rationale is explained","detail":"Butler used the law-of-war concept of enemy property liable to seizure. The argument denied labor to the rebellion without yet declaring the people legally free."},{"time":"7:37","audioTime":457,"title":"Custody is distinguished from emancipation","detail":"The men were retained by the United States and hired for work at the fort, but Butler’s initial decision did not itself create a comprehensive legal status of freedom."},{"time":"8:05","audioTime":485,"title":"Secession is turned against the return demand","detail":"Butler argued that Virginia could not claim to be a foreign country through secession and simultaneously demand enforcement of a United States fugitive-slave law."},{"time":"8:15","audioTime":495,"title":"Washington endorses Butler’s action","detail":"General-in-Chief Winfield Scott, Secretary of War Simon Cameron, and President Lincoln accepted the policy of not surrendering the freedom seekers."},{"time":"8:31","audioTime":511,"title":"Cameron leaves final status unresolved","detail":"The War Department authorized protection and labor while reserving the people’s ultimate legal disposition for later federal action."},{"time":"9:16","audioTime":556,"title":"The contraband decision becomes a policy precedent","detail":"Other commanders copied Butler’s practice because it weakened Confederate labor resources and added workers inside United States lines."},{"time":"9:53","audioTime":593,"title":"The House supports refuge","detail":"With Owen Lovejoy’s urging, the House resolved on July 9, 1861, that returning fugitives was not part of a United States soldier’s duty."},{"time":"10:14","audioTime":614,"title":"Congress later makes non-return a military rule","detail":"The March 13, 1862, Additional Article of War prohibited military and naval personnel from using forces to return people escaping claimed labor."},{"time":"10:26","audioTime":626,"title":"The First Confiscation Act is introduced","detail":"The August 6, 1861, law forfeited an enslaver’s claim when an enslaved person was required or permitted to support the rebellion."},{"time":"10:49","audioTime":649,"title":"The act’s limits are stated","detail":"The first act did not abolish slavery generally and covered only labor tied to support for the rebellion."},{"time":"11:10","audioTime":670,"title":"Fort Monroe’s refugee population expands","detail":"The arrival of women and children, as well as more men than the army needed for labor, exposed the inadequacy of a policy framed only around military employment."},{"time":"11:48","audioTime":708,"title":"Butler treats the refugees as free in practice","detail":"He concluded that the United States should not become their new owner and said they should never be reclaimed by former enslavers."},{"time":"13:14","audioTime":794,"title":"Field commanders claim broader authority","detail":"The discussion shifts from case-by-case protection to departmental emancipation proclamations issued without prior presidential authorization."},{"time":"13:20","audioTime":800,"title":"Frémont takes command in Missouri","detail":"John C. Frémont commanded the Department of the West during violent irregular warfare and political conflict in the loyal slave state."},{"time":"13:36","audioTime":816,"title":"Frémont declares martial law and emancipation","detail":"His August 30 proclamation threatened execution for captured guerrillas and freedom for enslaved people belonging to Confederate supporters."},{"time":"13:54","audioTime":834,"title":"Hunter’s Department of the South is defined","detail":"David Hunter’s jurisdiction covered South Carolina, Georgia, and Florida, although United States forces controlled only limited coastal areas."},{"time":"14:20","audioTime":860,"title":"Hunter declares departmental emancipation","detail":"General Orders No. 11 of May 9, 1862, declared enslaved people throughout the three-state department free."},{"time":"14:40","audioTime":880,"title":"Lincoln rejects unauthorized general emancipation","detail":"He distinguished Butler’s limited military seizure from a field commander independently changing national policy across an entire department."},{"time":"15:07","audioTime":907,"title":"Lincoln reserves the decision to the president","detail":"His May 19 proclamation declared Hunter’s emancipation provision void and stated that the question of military necessity belonged to presidential authority."},{"time":"15:22","audioTime":922,"title":"Border-state loyalty shapes presidential policy","detail":"Lincoln feared that Frémont’s order in Missouri could alienate Unionists in Kentucky and other loyal slave states."},{"time":"15:53","audioTime":953,"title":"Lincoln countermanded both generals","detail":"Frémont’s emancipation clause was narrowed to the First Confiscation Act; Hunter’s three-state emancipation provision was declared void."},{"time":"16:09","audioTime":969,"title":"Halleck excludes freedom seekers from lines","detail":"After replacing Frémont, Henry Halleck issued restrictive orders in the western theater that conflicted with more protective field practice."},{"time":"16:43","audioTime":1003,"title":"John A. Dix follows a restrictive course in Virginia","detail":"The hosts use Dix and Halleck to show that federal policy remained inconsistent across commands."},{"time":"17:11","audioTime":1031,"title":"Contradictory orders create policy ambiguity","detail":"Before the March 1862 law, freedom seekers could encounter shelter in one command and exclusion or return in another."},{"time":"17:34","audioTime":1054,"title":"Lincoln’s early policy is characterized as conservative","detail":"The episode contrasts his caution about national emancipation with Congress’s willingness to legislate against slaveholders supporting rebellion."},{"time":"18:24","audioTime":1104,"title":"War is already eroding slavery","detail":"Lincoln later described slavery as being worn down by the ordinary incidents of war even before a comprehensive presidential policy existed."},{"time":"18:54","audioTime":1134,"title":"Compensated emancipation becomes Lincoln’s border-state strategy","detail":"From late 1861 into July 1862, he urged loyal slave states to choose gradual abolition financed by the federal government."},{"time":"19:23","audioTime":1163,"title":"Lincoln presents compensation as a military measure","detail":"He argued that voluntary abolition in loyal slave states would remove Confederate hopes that those states might yet join the rebellion."},{"time":"20:29","audioTime":1229,"title":"The hosts question Lincoln’s strategic premise","detail":"The episode notes that no evidence shows disappointment over Maryland and Kentucky would have caused the seceded states to abandon the war."},{"time":"21:09","audioTime":1269,"title":"Lincoln sends his March proposal to Congress","detail":"The March 6 message asked Congress to promise financial cooperation with any state voluntarily adopting gradual abolition."},{"time":"21:15","audioTime":1275,"title":"Colonization accompanies gradualism and compensation","detail":"Lincoln paired the state-directed plan with voluntary emigration in an effort to reduce resistance among white slaveholders and northern voters."},{"time":"21:37","audioTime":1297,"title":"Congress approves, but the states do not act","detail":"Both houses adopted the joint resolution, yet none of the loyal slave-state legislatures accepted the program."},{"time":"21:47","audioTime":1307,"title":"Delaware declines to lead","detail":"Despite having only 1,798 enslaved people in the 1860 census, Delaware’s opposition prevented supporters from bringing a plan to a legislative vote."},{"time":"22:19","audioTime":1339,"title":"Lincoln makes a final appeal to border-state representatives","detail":"At the July 12 meeting he warned that continued war would destroy slavery without compensation and again requested an immediate decision for gradual abolition."},{"time":"23:10","audioTime":1390,"title":"A majority rejects immediate adoption","detail":"Most border-state representatives disputed Lincoln’s military argument and declined to endorse his proposal, though they left the matter to their states and constituents."},{"time":"23:19","audioTime":1399,"title":"The episode reaches the July 1862 policy break","detail":"Congress adjourned after enacting stronger confiscation legislation, and Lincoln moved toward a commander-in-chief emancipation measure."},{"time":"23:55","audioTime":1435,"title":"The book recommendation begins","detail":"The hosts recommend Eric Foner’s “The Fiery Trial: Abraham Lincoln and American Slavery.”"}],"command":[{"side":"United States civil and military authority","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"President Abraham Lincoln","command":"Commander in Chief and chief executive","subordinates":"Approved Butler’s limited field policy, ordered Frémont’s proclamation narrowed, voided Hunter’s proclamation, and proposed state-directed compensated emancipation"},{"commander":"Secretary of War Simon Cameron","command":"War Department through January 14, 1862","subordinates":"Approved Butler’s refusal to return freedom seekers and authorized their employment while leaving final legal status unresolved"},{"commander":"Secretary of War Edwin M. Stanton","command":"War Department from January 15, 1862","subordinates":"Led the department when Congress prohibited military returns and Lincoln revoked Hunter’s departmental emancipation order"},{"commander":"Lt. Gen. Winfield Scott","command":"General in Chief through November 1, 1861","subordinates":"Received Butler’s report from Fort Monroe and supported the decision to retain people used in Confederate military labor"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. George B. McClellan","command":"General in Chief, November 1, 1861–March 11, 1862","subordinates":"Held senior Army authority during part of the period; field policies nevertheless remained inconsistent across departments"}]},{"side":"United States field commands","color":"#8b5b25","branches":[{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Benjamin F. Butler","command":"Department of Virginia at Fort Monroe","subordinates":"Refused to return Baker, Mallory, and Townsend; retained and employed freedom seekers reaching United States lines"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John C. Frémont","command":"Department of the West","subordinates":"Issued the August 30, 1861, Missouri proclamation without prior presidential approval"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. Henry W. Halleck","command":"Department of the Missouri, later Department of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Replaced Frémont and issued orders excluding freedom seekers from military lines before the March 1862 statutory prohibition on returns"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. John A. Dix","command":"Departmental command in Virginia","subordinates":"Used restrictive policy toward freedom seekers, contributing to inconsistent treatment across United States commands"},{"commander":"Maj. Gen. David Hunter","command":"Department of the South","subordinates":"Declared enslaved people in South Carolina, Georgia, and Florida free; Lincoln voided that provision ten days later"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Frank Baker","role":"Self-emancipating man who escaped forced labor on Confederate fortifications and sought protection at Fort Monroe","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/building-1-quarters-no-1.htm"},{"name":"Shepard Mallory","role":"Self-emancipating man whose arrival at Fort Monroe helped force the first influential wartime federal decision on freedom seekers","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/building-1-quarters-no-1.htm"},{"name":"James Townsend","role":"Self-emancipating man who reached Fort Monroe with Baker and Mallory after forced Confederate military labor","side":"United States","source":"https://www.nps.gov/places/building-1-quarters-no-1.htm"},{"name":"Benjamin F. Butler","role":"Fort Monroe commander who refused to return the three men and developed the “contraband” rationale","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/april-1861-april-1862.html"},{"name":"Abraham Lincoln","role":"President who controlled national emancipation policy, balanced border-state loyalty against military necessity, and promoted compensated emancipation","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/abraham-lincoln-papers/articles-and-essays/abraham-lincoln-and-emancipation/"},{"name":"Simon Cameron","role":"Secretary of war who approved Butler’s protection and employment of freedom seekers in May 1861","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/april-1861-april-1862.html"},{"name":"Winfield Scott","role":"General in chief who supported Butler’s decision after receiving his report from Fort Monroe","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/april-1861-april-1862.html"},{"name":"Owen Lovejoy","role":"Illinois representative and abolitionist who urged the House resolution against military return of freedom seekers","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/abraham-lincoln-papers/articles-and-essays/abraham-lincoln-and-emancipation/timeline/"},{"name":"John C. Frémont","role":"Department of the West commander whose unauthorized Missouri emancipation clause Lincoln ordered narrowed","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/mal1159000/"},{"name":"David Hunter","role":"Department of the South commander whose unauthorized three-state emancipation order Lincoln voided","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/mal1604600/"},{"name":"Henry W. Halleck","role":"Frémont’s successor in Missouri who used restrictive orders toward freedom seekers before Congress established a uniform non-return rule","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/abraham-lincoln-papers/articles-and-essays/abraham-lincoln-and-emancipation/"},{"name":"Frederick Douglass","role":"Abolitionist who argued that the United States could not defeat a slaveholders’ rebellion while continuing to protect slavery","side":"United States","source":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/african-american-odyssey/civil-war.html"},{"name":"Charles K. Mallory","role":"Confederate colonel and enslaver who demanded that Baker, Mallory, and Townsend be returned","side":"Confederacy","source":"https://www.nps.gov/fomr/learn/management/upload/FOMR_FD_2015-3.pdf"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/ep202-policy-geography.svg","alt":"Schematic map of the military departments and loyal slave states central to the episode.","caption":"Field policy varied by place and commander: refuge at Fort Monroe, departmental proclamations in Missouri and the South Atlantic, and presidential caution focused on the loyal slave states.","source":"National Park Service, Library of Congress, Abraham Lincoln Papers, and episode transcript."},{"image":"/images/ep202-policy-timeline.svg","alt":"Timeline tracing self-emancipation, military action, legislation, and presidential policy from May 1861 to July 1862.","caption":"Self-emancipation, military policy, congressional legislation, and presidential action moved on related but distinct tracks.","source":"National Park Service, U.S. Senate Historical Office, Library of Congress, National Archives, and Abraham Lincoln’s messages."},{"image":"/images/ep202-fort-monroe-arrivals.jpg","alt":"An illustrated newspaper spread depicting African American freedom seekers arriving at Fort Monroe in 1861.","caption":"Frank Leslie’s Illustrated Newspaper represented the arrival of freedom seekers at Fort Monroe in its June 8, 1861, issue.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."},{"image":"/images/ep202-yorktown-contrabands.jpg","alt":"African American refugees and United States soldiers outside a farmhouse near Yorktown, Virginia, in May 1862.","caption":"James F. Gibson photographed African American refugees with United States soldiers at the headquarters of Gen. Lafayette near Yorktown in May 1862. The image records people living and working behind United States lines; it is not a photograph of the three men at Fort Monroe.","source":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division."}],"sources":[{"label":"Official Civil War Podcast episode 202 page","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/category/1862/page/5/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Fort Monroe and the Contrabands of War","url":"https://www.nps.gov/articles/fort-monroe-and-the-contrabands-of-war.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Quarters No. 1 and the May 1861 decision","url":"https://www.nps.gov/places/building-1-quarters-no-1.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Fort Monroe foundation document","url":"https://www.nps.gov/fomr/learn/management/upload/FOMR_FD_2015-3.pdf"},{"label":"National Archives: The Revolutionary Summer of 1862","url":"https://www.archives.gov/publications/prologue/2017/winter/summer-of-1862"},{"label":"Library of Congress: The Civil War in America, April 1861–April 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/exhibits/civil-war-in-america/april-1861-april-1862.html"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Abraham Lincoln and Emancipation","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/abraham-lincoln-papers/articles-and-essays/abraham-lincoln-and-emancipation/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: emancipation timeline","url":"https://www.loc.gov/collections/abraham-lincoln-papers/articles-and-essays/abraham-lincoln-and-emancipation/timeline/"},{"label":"U.S. Senate Historical Office: Confiscation Acts of 1861 and 1862","url":"https://www.senate.gov/artandhistory/history/common/generic/ConfiscationActs.htm"},{"label":"National Archives: text of the March 13 Additional Article of War","url":"https://www.archives.gov/exhibits/american_originals_iv/sections/transcript_preliminary_emancipation.html"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln orders Frémont’s proclamation modified, September 11, 1861","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/mal1159000/"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln revokes Hunter’s emancipation order, May 19, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/mal1604600/"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln recommends compensated emancipation, March 6, 1862","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/message-congress-recommending-compensated-emancipation"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln appeals to border-state representatives, July 12, 1862","url":"https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/documents/message-appeal-border-state-representatives-favor-compensated-emancipation"},{"label":"Primary source: majority response of border-state representatives, July 14, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/mal.1708800/?st=text"},{"label":"Primary source: Lincoln’s earliest draft proclamation, July 22, 1862","url":"https://www.loc.gov/resource/mal.1723200/?st=text"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau: national enslaved population in 1860","url":"https://www.census.gov/about/history/stories/monthly/2025/june-2025.html"},{"label":"U.S. Census Bureau: 1860 slave-distribution map and state table","url":"https://www2.census.gov/about/history/historical-censuses-surveys/survey-census-history/geography/1860-slave-distribution.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress: 1861 Fortress Monroe arrivals engraving","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/99614015/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: African American refugees near Yorktown photograph","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2014646902/"},{"label":"Official podcast book-recommendations list","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/book-recommendations/"}]}}]}]
11:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section maps-section","id":"maps","children":[["$","h2",null,{"children":"Maps"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"maps-grid","children":[["$","figure","Arrival at Fortress Monroe",{"className":"reference-map","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"map-frame static-map-frame","children":["$","a",null,{"className":"map-image-link","href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep202-fort-monroe-arrivals.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","aria-label":"Open Arrival at Fortress Monroe at full size","children":["$","img",null,{"src":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep202-fort-monroe-arrivals.jpg","alt":"An 1861 newspaper engraving showing African American freedom seekers arriving at Fort Monroe and related scenes around the fort.","width":1600,"height":1200,"loading":"lazy"}]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"Arrival at Fortress Monroe"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Frank Leslie’s Illustrated Newspaper published this composite engraving on June 8, 1861. Its central scene depicts African American freedom seekers entering Fort Monroe, while surrounding scenes show movement, reception, work, and camp life. It is a contemporary illustrated-news representation, not a photograph of the May 23 arrival."}],["$","span",null,{"className":"map-actions","children":[["$","a",null,{"href":"/projects/civil-war-field-companion/images/ep202-fort-monroe-arrivals.jpg","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Open full-size map ",["$","$Le",null,{"ref":"$undefined","iconNode":"$d:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:children:1:props:children:2:props:iconNode","className":"lucide-arrow-up-right","size":13}]]}],["$","small",null,{"children":"Library of Congress Prints and Photographs Division; see sources below."}]]}],"$undefined"]}]]}]]}]]}]
